d  for it quite well beating out everyone  else  so how do they do on snjs code they're  great on that are they great on  everything else  they're close on some things farther on  others so safari is in light blue  and like before lower numbers are better  the numbers for firefox and chrome are  basically the same as before you can see  on primes for example and on copy and  corrections they're pretty close but on  several others they're a bit farther  and in fact it's possible to see what  the issue is so on memops  there's several times slower memphis  literally all it does is just hammer at  memory just reads reads reads writes  writes writes it doesn't do many  computations  on it doesn't do actual math it just  reads and writes basically  so they've just not optimized typed  arrays  to the extent that other browsers have  but it's not that hard to do so  hopefully that'll happen  and they're not standing still either  they're actually experimenting with  using  lvm as egypt which is pretty exciting  because no  other javascript engine has ever tried  this  and potentially it could give them very  large speedups  we'll just have to see how the project  goes  a few words about internet explorer  so ie6 was not a great browser  ie 7 and 8 got better 910  11 actually looks promising so it's in  pre-release i'm not going to show you  numbers because it's not the final  version i don't think it's fair to  measure but  it looks pretty good i've seen it beat  other browsers on a few things  it supports webgl which is huge first of  all for the standards process that they  finally  that they're finally playing ball with  with the webgl standard which there was  some  it was unclear whether they would so  that's great but it's also great  because webgl was the reason for the  invention of javascript typed arrays  they were basically a way to give binary  data to  uh to javascript for purposes of  graphics and textures turned out to be  useful for other things like  smgs 